"""
==============================================================================
PORTFOLIO RISK SYNTHESIZER
An agentic RAG system that analyzes investment portfolios for concentration
risk, sector imbalance, and regulatory red flags using LangGraph + RAG.
==============================================================================

WHY THIS PROJECT (for interviews):
    Most "RAG chatbot" projects are a single retrieve -> generate chain.
    This one is a multi-step AGENT: it retrieves evidence from fund
    documents + regulatory text, runs deterministic risk checks, branches
    based on what it finds (a real conditional edge, not just decoration),
    and only then asks an LLM to synthesize everything into a report that
    is grounded in the retrieved evidence and the computed numbers.

ARCHITECTURE (LangGraph state machine):

    parse_portfolio
          |
          v
    retrieve_context  <-- RAG: TF-IDF retrieval over fund docs + regulation docs
          |
          v
    run_risk_checks    <-- deterministic: concentration + sector concentration
          |
          v
    regulatory_check   <-- RAG-grounded: matches holdings against retrieved
          |               regulation snippets
          v
    [conditional edge] -- if any HIGH severity flag -> escalate_node
          |                                              |
          | (else)                                       v
          v                                        escalate_node
    synthesize_report  <-------------------------------- |
          |
          v
         END

WHY TF-IDF INSTEAD OF NEURAL EMBEDDINGS:
    A deliberate engineering trade-off, not a limitation I was unaware of.
    Fund documents use precise, repeated vocabulary (fund names, sector
    labels, regulation clause numbers) where lexical overlap retrieval
    (TF-IDF / cosine similarity) is fast, needs no model download or GPU,
    and is easy to reason about and unit-test. In an interview you can say:
    "I used TF-IDF retrieval because the domain vocabulary is precise and
    it kept the system dependency-light; swapping in HuggingFaceEmbeddings
    or OpenAIEmbeddings behind the same VectorStore interface is a one
    line change -- see `SimpleVectorStore`." That's a legitimate, defensible
    answer to "why didn't you use real embeddings?"

WHY THE LLM STEP IS OPTIONAL (GRACEFUL DEGRADATION):
    The report-synthesis node calls Anthropic's API if ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is
    set, and otherwise falls back to a deterministic template built from
    the same evidence. This means the whole pipeline is always demoable
    live, with or without an API key, and it's a legitimate production
    pattern (never let an optional enrichment step take down the pipeline).

SETUP:
    pip install langgraph langchain-core anthropic scikit-learn numpy
    export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-...      # optional, enables LLM narrative

RUN:
    python portfolio_risk_synthesizer.py            # demo on one portfolio
    python portfolio_risk_synthesizer.py --evaluate  # run the test suite
==============================================================================
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Any, Literal, TypedDict

import numpy as np
from langchain_core.documents import Document
from langgraph.graph import END, StateGraph
from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer
from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity

